Local changes to phhusson/ims (floss-ims), for VoWiFi on a Redmi K50 Ultra
running a ported HyperOS / Android 17 with a UK VOXI eSIM.

Base:  https://github.com/phhusson/ims  commit c180bdf ("Handle having require
       being on multiple lines", 2025-01-15)
       NOTE the repo is phhusson/ims -- phhusson/floss-ims is a 404.
Apply: patch -p1 < floss-ims-local.patch      (from the repo root)

Verified: applies with zero fuzz to c180bdf and round-trips to the exact source
these APKs were built from. State as of v43.

Maintenance scope (2026-09-13): registration and SMS. The user reports current
incoming and outgoing call failures; historical voice results below are not a
current voice-availability guarantee.

HISTORICAL TEST RESULTS with this patch: SIP registration over a real ePDG tunnel, SMS in
both directions, and outgoing VOICE CALLS (dialled 191, connected, two-way AMR
audio, correct call timer). Incoming calls are code-reviewed and hardened but
were broken until v42 -- see below -- and the fix is not yet re-tested with a
real inbound call.

=== Voice calls ===
  * SipHandler: fix the RFC 3312 precondition handshake. In a response the peer
    sends us, "local" is the PEER and "remote" is US -- upstream read them the
    other way round and only acted when the peer reported ITS OWN side as not
    ready. Vodafone's first 183 already says "a=curr:qos local sendrecv" plus
    "a=conf:qos remote sendrecv" (I am ready, now confirm you are), so upstream
    answered nothing and the network failed the call with 580 Precondition
    Failure. This is what stopped calls from working at all.
  * SipHandler: build the precondition UPDATE from OUR OWN SDP. Upstream edited
    the peer's SDP and sent it back, so the UPDATE advertised the NETWORK's
    connection address and RTP port as ours -> 403 Forbidden. Also drop Route
    (in-dialog request) and the registration-shaped Expires.
  * PhhMmTelFeature + SipHandler: report an answered OUTGOING call to the
    framework (new onCallConnected -> callSessionInitiated). Only incoming calls
    ever published a listener, so Telecom kept outgoing calls at DIALING for
    their whole duration and logged duration=0 -- a stale entry then sat in
    dumpsys telecom looking like a call still in progress.
  * SipHandler: stopCallMedia() tears the media path down on every exit path
    (failure, CANCEL, BYE, reject, terminate). Codec threads start on the 183,
    well before the call is answered, and upstream's failure path only notified
    the framework -- so each failed call stranded an AudioRecord, two MediaCodecs
    and the RTP reader. Closing rtpSocket is the load-bearing part: the decode
    thread blocks in receive() and would never reach its own stop check.
  * SipHandler: media threads release in a finally. They normally exit BY
    throwing (socket closed), so releasing after the loop never ran -- leaving
    the microphone held for the life of the process.
  * SipHandler: log the RTP stream once, not once per packet. Upstream logged
    two lines per 20ms frame (~100 lines/s, 19744 for one call), which buries
    the rest of the radio log and is indistinguishable from the main-socket spin
    failure below -- our own watchdog read it as a spin and force-stopped a
    working call.

  * SipHandler: reset callStopped/callStarted at the start of an OUTGOING call.
    handleCall (incoming) always did this; the outgoing path never has, which did
    not matter until every teardown path started calling stopCallMedia(). The
    flag then survived the previous call, so the NEXT call's encode thread saw it
    already set and skipped opening the microphone: the call connected with audio
    one way only. Symptom is "connected but no sound", and only from the second
    call onwards -- the first call after a restart is always fine, which makes it
    easy to misread as a network problem. (v43)

=== Two bugs that broke real calls, found 2026-08-23 22:2x (v42) ===
  * SipHandler: the SDP o= line in the INCOMING-call answer was malformed.
    Upstream emitted "o=<imsi> 1 2 IN IP4 <addr>" -- five fields where RFC 4566
    requires six (username, sess-id, sess-version, nettype, addrtype, address).
    Vodafone replied to our 183 with
    CANCEL Reason: SIP;cause=200;text="Invalid SDP" about 2s after it rang, so
    EVERY incoming call dropped the moment it appeared, and the caller saw "call
    failed". Now "o=- 1 2 ..." like the two outgoing builders that already work.
  * SipHandler: outgoing calls to full international numbers lost the leading +.
    The framework hands the number over already stripped ("44xxxxxxxxxx") and
    upstream passed it straight into tel:, which RFC 3966 reads as a LOCAL
    number -- Vodafone answered 404 Not Found. The + is restored only for
    all-digit numbers of 11+ digits not starting with 0, so short codes (191)
    and national-format numbers (07...) are left alone. Both cases re-tested.

=== Incoming calls / crash-safety (v40-v41) ===
  * SipHandler: rejectCall() tears the media down. handleCall starts the codec
    threads while the phone is still ringing (it must, for early media), so
    declining a call leaked a whole pipeline.
  * SipHandler: acceptCall/rejectCall no longer do `currentCall!!`. Both run on
    their own thread, where an NPE is silent: the caller can hang up first, and
    the result is a call that never connects with nothing logged.
  * SipHandler: handleUpdate answers 481 instead of throwing when no call is in
    progress. It runs on the control-socket reader, so an NPE there kills the
    loop that carries the whole registration.
  * PhhMmTelFeature: reject()/terminate() report callSessionTerminated, so the
    incoming-call UI does not hang after the user declines.
  * SipHandler: BOTH media threads are bare thread{} bodies, so an escaping
    exception is a process-killing FATAL EXCEPTION, not a caught teardown. The
    encode thread's pre-answer keepalive loop and the decode thread's
    `currentCall!!` were OUTSIDE the try -- hanging up during early media crashed
    the whole process and took the SIP registration with it (reproduced on v40,
    fixed and re-tested on v41). Both are now inside, and the mic is never
    opened if the call ended while ringing.

=== Registration reliability ===
  * SipHandler: honour parseMessage()'s return value on the main/control socket.
    Upstream discards it, so when the P-CSCF closes the connection (hourly here)
    the reader spun at 100% CPU forever and the registration went dead while the
    process stayed alive.
  * SipHandler: reconnect after the peer closes the control socket, guarded by a
    generation counter (stale readers cannot rebuild) and exponential backoff
    (2s..120s, reset after 10min of stability).
  * SipHandler: releaseConnection() frees the sockets, SPIs and IPsec transforms
    of the previous attempt -- upstream never reconnects so it never frees them,
    and sec-agree SAs carry no lifetime, so the kernel never expires them.
    Also called on both connect() failure paths and at connect() entry.
  * SipHandler: connect() serialised behind a lock -- three threads reach it.
  * SipHandler: the two server-socket reader loops capture their socket and stop
    on generation change, instead of re-reading fields connect() reassigns (an
    old loop would serve the NEW listener and race the fresh reader).
  * PhhImsService: refresh registration at half the expiry the network actually
    granted (Vodafone UK grants 3590s; upstream's fixed 3000s alarm plus MIUI's
    alarm slack let the binding lapse, silently dropping incoming SMS).

=== Platform / HyperOS ===
  * SipConnection: SipConnectionTcpServer built on a raw FileDescriptor via
    android.system.Os. Android 17 removed the hidden getFileDescriptor$ method
    upstream reflected on, and the NIO channel's fd field is blocklisted.
    accept() uses that FD; close() closes it (the ServerSocket is a placeholder).
  * SipConnection: Tcp/Udp close() also close their own IPsec transforms.
  * PhhImsService: declare features via querySupportedImsFeatures() rather than
    manifest metadata.
  * PhhMmTelFeature / PhhMmTelFeatureProtected: HyperOS's
    notifyCapabilitiesStatusChanged differs from AOSP; the Java shim reaches the
    protected member without tripping the hidden-API blocklist.
  * Sms / SipMessage: SMSC field decoding (TS 24.011) and header parsing fixes.

Not in this patch (packaging, not source): _fix/manifest/AndroidManifest.xml,
_fix/pack_apk.py, _fix/build_floss_v27.sh. Bring those too -- targetSdk 28 (for
the MAX-TARGET-O hidden APIs) and RECORD_AUDIO live there, and calls need both.

